WEBVTT
TITLE Integration Techniques And Definite Integrals — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A definite integral is the area under a curve, counted with sign.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
Left, right, midpoint and trapezoid differ only in the sample height.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
Ten times as many panels cuts the left-rule error by about ten.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
The integral is the limit, and the limit is what the theorem evaluates.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
The accumulation function differentiates back to the integrand.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
The second half turns an area into one subtraction.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Three standard integrals, evaluated.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
The table is small, and every technique reduces to it.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Additivity: a middle point splits the integral into two.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Reversal: swapping the limits flips the sign.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
Linearity: constants come out and sums split.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
Symmetry: an odd integrand cancels over a symmetric interval.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Substitution is the chain rule read backwards.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
The limits must be rewritten in the new variable.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
The two routes agree exactly, not approximately.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
The differential has to be replaced as well as the variable.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
By parts comes from the product rule.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
LIATE orders the choices when both parts are plausible.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
The boundary term carries the whole answer here.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
Repeated by parts has a tabular form.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
A rational integrand is split into standard pieces.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
Repeated factors need every power in the split.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
Derivative over denominator gives a logarithm.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
Long division first, then the split.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
The average value of a function is its integral over the width.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
A continuous function reaches its average value somewhere.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
For x squared on the interval from 0 to 3, c is the square root of 3.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
Without continuity there need not be such a point.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
An infinite limit of integration is handled by a limit.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
The p-test settles the whole family at once.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
The logarithm integral diverges, but only logarithmically.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
An integrand that blows up can still enclose a finite area.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
A density is a function whose integral is one.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Expectation is the integral of x against the density.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Uniform and normal, checked by the same three integrals.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
A sum over samples is an approximation, not the expectation.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
First ask which of the four question types this is.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
For evaluation, classify the integrand before choosing a technique.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Four errors are offered as options, and three are recognisable.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Check the answer against the picture before you commit.

